00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,195
Slide 7 compares POWERTOYS RUN VS.

2
00:00:04,195 --> 00:00:07,014
GOOGLE APP: Native C++ vs.

3
00:00:07,014 --> 00:00:09,400
1.2GB Chromium Shell.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:12,995
Look at this benchmark comparison, students!

5
00:00:12,995 --> 00:00:15,682
On the left: Microsoft PowerToys Run.

6
00:00:15,682 --> 00:00:18,224
It is written in native C++ and C#.

7
00:00:18,224 --> 00:00:29,410
It uses just 35MB of RAM, launches in 4 milliseconds, and does local math, app launching, and unit conversions without sending a single byte to the cloud!

8
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:33,711
But on the right: The Google App for Windows!

9
00:00:33,711 --> 00:00:40,300
It consumes over 1,200MB of RAM—that is 34 times more memory than PowerToys!

10
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:41,900
Why?

11
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:47,928
Because Google shoved a complete Chromium browser engine into that search bar!

12
00:00:47,928 --> 00:00:54,380
On a 16GB laptop running Photoshop and VS Code, that 1.2GB footprint causes noticeable stutter!

13
00:00:54,780 --> 00:01:04,600
But to be fair, Google gives you Gemini Flash multimodal intelligence, live screen translation, and cloud synthesis that PowerToys cannot do on its own!

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,200
That is the fundamental architectural trade-off: lean local responsiveness versus heavy cloud intelligence.

